                    <span class="text-accent font-mono mr-3">01.</span> Project Overview
                </h2>
                <div class="prose prose-invert max-w-none text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
                    <p>
                        Debugging embedded communication buses typically requires expensive benchtop oscilloscopes or PC-tethered USB logic analyzers. To create a portable, self-contained alternative, I engineered a dual-microcontroller workbench that decouples deterministic signal generation from high-speed digital waveform capture.
                    </p>
                    <p class="mt-4">
                        On one side, an <strong>ESP32-D0WD-V3</strong> serves as an interactive target stimulus generator, outputting live I2C display frames, UART telemetry packets, simulated SPI sensor bursts, and adjustable LEDC PWM square waves controlled via a KY-040 rotary encoder menu. On the other side, an <strong>ESP32-S3</strong> acts as a 4-channel pocket logic analyzer, capturing logic edges via single-cycle GPIO register reads and rendering color-coded waveforms alongside decoded I2C addresses, UART ASCII text, and PWM frequency/duty telemetry on a 1.44-inch ST7735 TFT display.
                    </p>
                </div>
