                    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 flex items-center">
                        <span class="text-accent font-mono mr-3">03.</span> Real-Time Protocol Decoding
                    </h2>
                    <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
                        Rather than displaying raw square waves alone, the ESP32-S3 analyzer runs multi-protocol software decoders over every 512-sample capture buffer. It detects I2C START/STOP conditions to extract the 7-bit slave address (<code class="font-mono text-accent bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">0x3C</code>), measures minimum pulse widths on CH3 to auto-detect baud rate and reconstruct 8N1 UART ASCII characters, and averages cycle periods on CH2 to compute live PWM frequency and duty cycle percentages.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 flex items-center">
                        <span class="text-accent font-mono mr-3">04.</span> Software &amp; Tools
                    </h2>
                    <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                        Both firmwares were developed in <strong>C++</strong> targeting the <strong>ESP32 Arduino Core v3.0+</strong> in the <strong>Arduino IDE</strong>.
                    </p>
                    <ul class="space-y-2 text-slate-400">
                        <li><i class="fas fa-check text-accent mr-2"></i> Migrated PWM generation to Core v3 <code class="font-mono text-accent bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">ledcAttach()</code> and <code class="font-mono text-accent bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">ledcWrite()</code> APIs.</li>
                        <li><i class="fas fa-check text-accent mr-2"></i> Non-blocking <code class="font-mono text-accent bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">millis()</code> task scheduler for zero-latency rotary encoder polling.</li>
                        <li><i class="fas fa-check text-accent mr-2"></i> Free-running oscilloscope sweep tuned to a 500 ms refresh cadence.</li>
                    </ul>
