            <!-- Challenges & Solutions -->
            <section>
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-8 flex items-center">
                    <span class="text-accent font-mono mr-3">05.</span> Challenges &amp; Solutions
                </h2>
                
                <div class="space-y-8">
                    <!-- Challenge 1 -->
                    <div class="bg-dark p-6 md:p-8 rounded-xl border border-slate-800 relative overflow-hidden group">
                        <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-1 h-full bg-red-500/50 group-hover:bg-red-500 transition-colors"></div>
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3 flex items-center">
                            <i class="fas fa-exclamation-triangle text-red-400 mr-3"></i> 
                            Blocking Loop Latency &amp; Missed Encoder Detents
                        </h3>
                        <p class="text-slate-400 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
                            <strong class="text-slate-300">The Problem:</strong> Initial firmware revisions used a <code class="font-mono text-accent bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">delay(200)</code> call at the end of the main loop to pace UART and OLED updates. Because the CPU stalled for 200 ms every cycle, rapid turns on the KY-040 rotary encoder went unregistered and failed to update the PWM output or OLED cursor.
                        </p>
                        <div class="bg-slate-900 p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-700">
                            <h4 class="text-accent font-mono text-sm mb-2"><i class="fas fa-wrench mr-2"></i>The Solution</h4>
                            <p class="text-slate-400 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                                Refactored the target generator into a non-blocking <code class="font-mono text-accent bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">millis()</code> state machine. Polling <code class="font-mono text-accent bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">handleEncoder()</code> continuously at full loop speed while gating bus bursts to 200 ms intervals restored instant mechanical responsiveness and live OLED feedback.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Challenge 2 -->
                    <div class="bg-dark p-6 md:p-8 rounded-xl border border-slate-800 relative overflow-hidden group">
                        <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-1 h-full bg-yellow-500/50 group-hover:bg-yellow-500 transition-colors"></div>
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3 flex items-center">
                            <i class="fas fa-tachometer-alt text-yellow-400 mr-3"></i> 
                            Balancing Trigger Arming vs. Oscilloscope Sweep Pacing
                        </h3>
                        <p class="text-slate-400 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
                            <strong class="text-slate-300">The Problem:</strong> Edge-triggered capture sweeps with a 1500 ms display hold felt sluggish during live frequency tuning, whereas stripping the trigger wait with a 150 ms refresh rate caused the waveforms to cycle too rapidly to read telemetry comfortably.
                        </p>
                        <div class="bg-slate-900 p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-700">
                            <h4 class="text-accent font-mono text-sm mb-2"><i class="fas fa-wrench mr-2"></i>The Solution</h4>
                            <p class="text-slate-400 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                                Implemented a free-running immediate acquisition sweep paired with a tuned <strong>500 ms refresh interval</strong> (<code class="font-mono text-accent bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">REFRESH_INTERVAL_MS 500</code>) and a 5 ms static-bus timeout. This eliminated arming stalls while maintaining a readable 2 Hz oscilloscope cadence alongside instant single-button frame freeze (<code class="font-mono text-accent bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">HOLD</code>).
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Challenge 3 -->
                    <div class="bg-dark p-6 md:p-8 rounded-xl border border-slate-800 relative overflow-hidden group">
                        <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-1 h-full bg-cyan-500/50 group-hover:bg-cyan-500 transition-colors"></div>
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3 flex items-center">
                            <i class="fas fa-code-branch text-cyan-400 mr-3"></i> 
                            ESP32 Arduino Core v3.0+ LEDC API Migration
                        </h3>
                        <p class="text-slate-400 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
                            <strong class="text-slate-300">The Problem:</strong> Compiling legacy <code class="font-mono text-accent bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">ledcSetup()</code> and <code class="font-mono text-accent bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">ledcAttachPin()</code> calls under ESP32 Arduino Core v3.x triggered scope compilation errors due to breaking peripheral API changes.
                        </p>
                        <div class="bg-slate-900 p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-700">
                            <h4 class="text-accent font-mono text-sm mb-2"><i class="fas fa-wrench mr-2"></i>The Solution</h4>
                            <p class="text-slate-400 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                                Updated the PWM driver to use the unified <code class="font-mono text-accent bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">ledcAttach(PWM_PIN, freq, 8)</code> and pin-addressed <code class="font-mono text-accent bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">ledcWrite(PWM_PIN, dutyVal)</code> functions, enabling dynamic runtime reconfiguration of both frequency and duty cycle.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

